Arby as a political spectacle it was  hard to beat pomp circumstance and  mutual admiration on the leader of the  most powerful country and a man who  wants to be a player on the world stage  but the relationship between Donald  Trump and India's Prime Minister  Narendra Modi is about more than just  compliments it's about trade money and  it's about China s perspective towards  India is based on location geography and  economic potential if you take all these  into account there's no other country in  Asia which can offer any kind of a  balance to China the United States sees  India as an essential buffer to the  growing expansionism of China in the  Asia region the US has the most powerful  Navy in the world but India is the  dominant power in the Indian Ocean and  there is concern that Beijing may spread  its power in the South China Sea to  India's area of control it sees itself  and it models itself on America on that  ability to project power across the  oceans to be a global player to have to  be able to influence outcomes and  distant shores and distant parts of the  world so to that extent China is almost  certainly going to expand its going to  foray into the Indian Ocean and the  Indian Navy will have to contend with  that more than 40% of the world's oil  passes through the Indian Ocean is the  lifeline for the economies of many  countries China has developed bases and  ports in East Africa and Sri Lanka it is  currently operating nine naval ships in  the Indian Ocean but there are concerns  this may increase India is an essential  early warning system with a string of  strategic listening posts in the region  India has just agreed to purchase three  billion dollars worth of attack  helicopters from the US but it's  military capability is far below that of  China which spends four times more on  defense in addition the Indian Navy is  in need of upgrading it has just two  aircraft carriers for example compared  to China's 10 and is scaling back plans  to expand its fleet because of costs for  India having a powerful friend like the  United States is important but it  doesn't want to make an enemy out of  China either the two countries fought a  order war in 1962 over disputed  territory and there are still unresolved  issues but India is well aware of the  strength and capability of Asia's  growing superpower too much of the  Indian public it seems the Indian Prime  Minister got more out of the Trump visit  but critics say it was simply kudos and  endorsement the real substance belongs  to the US with India having to tow an  American line that previous Indian  governments have resisted such as how it  deals with Iran the options are  shrinking because Trump is also making  it a world outside the Chinese influence  where it is unipolar you will if America  has a problem with Iran you will not  deal with Iran even the India of 20-30  years of a beaker far weaker  economically would have been able to  play the Russians against the Americans  and continue dealing with Iran they  can't do it today so they are they have  to face the Chinese threat and they then  have to do whatever America requires of  them and that is actually the current  situation in which we are operating  India is having to perform a delicate  balancing act but as the US and China  continue their battle for global  dominance its hoping it won't become  collateral damage Tony Berkeley al  Jazeera Delhi
 

